  "text": {
   "approach": "Approach the same as the Main Peak's South Route to 'the Y', then turn up Gully #1 and follow it to the notch below the spire. Midway are 20 ft rock steps, climbable directly or bypassed via loose, brushy scrambling.",
   "approach_logistics": null,
   "road": "{\"name\":\"SE Middle Fork Snoqualmie River Road (FR 56)\",\"status\":\"Paved for most of its length past the Taylor River bridge junction; the last stretch to the South Route pull-off is rough and rutted. FSR 56 has a washout at MP 17.3 per the Forest's July 2026 road report, upstream of the Garfield turnoff.\",\"driveNote\":\"I-90 Exit 34 near North Bend, 0.3-0.4 mi on 468th Ave SE/Edgewick Rd, right on SE Middle Fork Rd (FR 56). The South Route approach starts from a pull-off roughly 2.6 miles beyond the Taylor River bridge, by a gravel wash — about an hour from Seattle.\",\"seasonalGate\":\"No gate, but ",
   "overview": "The original 1941 first-ascent line up Leaning Spire, a distinct 5,169 ft tower immediately west of Garfield's true summit.",
   "description": null,
   "descent": "Descend the line of ascent. A single 70 m rope handles it (60 m would probably work) via five or more rappels off trees and horns, linked by long stretches of downscrambling in the gully. Rappelling off the overhang near the summit into Gully #2 has been done, but getting out to that rappel is described as quite scary and it is not recommended. Below the Y Notch you are back on the Main Peak's Sou"
  },
